WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:14.600 align:start position:0%
 
do you pray with me  Oh God may the words of my mouth and the  meditations of each of our hearts be  acceptable in your sight  you are our rock and our Redeemer amen  case you haven't noticed we are in a  campaign season here in Connecticut and  the sport of wrestling has played a more  prominent role than perhaps ever before  but instead of talking about wrestling  as a major entertainment business I'd  like to talk about it as a way of being  faithful as Christians just so we don't  get these to confuse the professional  sport and the spiritual practice I'm  going to talk about it more frequently  this morning as struggling struggling is  a more appealing word for me at least I  can't stand one moment of violence on TV  and struggling it's something that most  of us experienced at some point in our  lives regardless of our athletic  abilities how is struggling a spiritual  practice that can be helpful to  Christians we take as our example this  morning Jacob the story in the book of  Genesis which Ron just read in which  Jacob encounters a figure in the night  and the two wrestle until it is almost  morning  we don't know many of the details but we  do know that Jacob leading up to this  mysterious encounter had been traveling  with his whole family traveling toward  his brother who he had not seen in years  this brother Esau whom Jacob wronged by  stealing their father's blessing this  brother who vowed to kill Jacob this  brother who was coming toward Jacob with  400 men Jacob was wrestling long before  his encounter with this stranger in the  night and then suddenly someone appears  and Jacob is terrified and has to  send himself we never know if Jacob  first assumes that this is his brother  we don't know if the two exchanged words  during the long nights struggle but as  the struggle is coming to an end  Jacob reveals his preoccupation his  motivation to endure this long night of  wrestling I will not let you go until  you bless me he says Jacob's father's  blessing which he stole from his brother  Esau was now a burden of guilt and the  blessings he had received from God  between men and now were impossible for  him to accept because he had done so  much wrong he wanted a new blessing a  real one turns out that Jacob had been  wrestling with God that night in some  form and the blessing is given just as  morning is breaking jacob is given a new  name it is Israel which means wrestles  with God  of course Jacob doesn't leave this scene  unchanged in other ways either he is  given a new name that sign of God's  blessing but he is also given a limp  late in the struggle Jacob was winning  and his opponent struck him in the hip  and put it out of joint  so Jacob leaves this place exhausted  from the long nights struggle I'm sure  as well as blessed and changed  I have fan on the sidelines of such  wrestling matches you have to these  intense spiritual struggles I think of a  young guy preparing to come out to his  parents as gay  he has already been struggling a long  time alone coming to terms with his  identity struggling to tell a friend  struggling with his church's traditional  teachings and now anticipating a  struggle as he shares with  his parents yet somehow he wants the  struggle he needs the struggle because  he's so badly wants the blessing of his  parents and I think of a young couple  finally expecting a child after many  years of hoping only to struggle through  miscarriage and heartbreak they wrestle  with God trying to form words to express  their disappointment and sadness and  anger it is a long night perhaps a whole  season of struggling they so badly want  a blessing from God if not a child then  the blessing of enough strength to  endure this kind of pain I think of the  33 Chilean miners trapped underground  for weeks and weeks and I have to wonder  what kind of wrestling they did how do  you honestly talk to God about life and  death about the small annoying things  and the big cosmic questions when you  are literally living in a dark night of  the soul for so long in a miracle of  technology we got to watch as they  received blessings first a tiny  connection with the world above ground  through that small tube in exchange of  food and letters and then this week  watching as one by one they were reborn  above-ground alive miraculously more  than alive  it seems our spiritual struggles often  parallel Jacob's wrestling in the night  we emerge exhausted from all the  struggling and yet we struggle for a  blessing and we emerge from the struggle  changed our gospel story today also  tells of a struggle a struggle of  endurance by a widow who so believed she  deserved justice that she approached a  judge over and over and over again  defying all of the power structures in  that society approaching a judge who  could care less really  but she finally wore him down he finally  granted her justice just so she would  stop bothering him our persistence our  pesky nests is not the point of this  parable Luke emphasizes that God is not  like the mean judge instead Jesus says  God will grant justice God will not  delay  Jesus urges us then to pray day and  night and God will quickly answer these  prayers yet we know our spiritual  struggles continue and I'm afraid that  we rarely understand these struggles to  be faithful more often I hear people  talk about spiritual struggles as a sign  of weakness and blame the bad things  happening in their lives to a lack of  prayer a lack of persistence not praying  enough or in the right ways so where  does this story of Jacob wrestling all  night long for a blessing meet the  promise in Luke that God will come  quickly and answer us well hopefully  these stories meet in our lives these  stories meet when we pray to Jesus and  don't see fast results when we struggle  with the tough realities of our lives  wondering if blessing will ever ever  come and yet I am convinced that  struggling with God is one way of being  faithful when Jesus invites us to pray  he asks us to pray honestly and if we  are being honest with God we've got to  struggle with God sometimes struggling  prayers usually begin with the question  why God why is this happening  God why isn't this happening Jesus why  aren't you Jesus why haven't you  and then sometimes these struggle  prayers run out of words  maybe Jacobs wrestling story doesn't  report words during the night because  there weren't any the two just had to  wrestle  until finally Jacob could name what he  really wanted a blessing from God  struggling with God is faithful work but  it is still an awful lot of work and yet  it is part of life so don't hide your  struggles from God  don't edit the struggles out of your  prayers God wants to share in these  struggles especially on days when we  celebrate communion like today we  remember that God invites us all to  bring our struggles to this table even  if those struggles are about God God can  handle it the way to this table is paved  by struggle Jesus gathered a table that  night on the night in which he was  betrayed talk about struggle the  beginning of Jesus struggle from life to  the grave and back to life again this is  a table that celebrates Jesus victory in  the struggle from life to death and back  to life this is a table that represents  God's welcome to each one of us who  struggle for justice here we receive a  meal of blessing the blessing of  nourishment after a long night of  wrestling the blessing of God's  community gathered and the promise that  we will never leave this table quite the  same you who struggle rest here for a  moment  receive abundant blessing be changed  amen

